.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PATH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_path \- return the URL path of a request
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char *chttpsvr_req_path(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the URL path of the request, without the query string.  That
string ends with a NUL byte.  It stays valid for the length of the
handler call.

The path always begins with "/".  The server answers a request whose
target is neither an origin-form path nor an absolute-form http or
https URL with 400 Bad Request, before any middleware runs.  The one
exception is "OPTIONS *", which the server answers itself, with no
middleware and no handler; see chttpsvr_start(3).  For an
absolute-form target the path is the path of that URL, and an empty
one is "/".

The library decodes the percent-encoded sequences under the path rules
of RFC 3986.  The string is therefore the path that the library
matched a route pattern against.  A middleware that runs for a
rejected request can reach a path whose percent-encoding is malformed.
See chttpsvr_use(3).  That malformed encoding is itself one of the
reasons why no route could match the path.  Such a path has no decoded
form, so this function gives it back exactly as it arrived, in its
encoded form.

The decoded path can hold a '/' that arrived as %2F, and "." and ".."
segments, which the server neither merges nor removes.  Never use it
as a file name without checking it.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_raw_query (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_param (3)
